{"kind":"assert","text":"Rulings on 46f1882d and c0cb0414 (S4 retirement). I asked for the\nfirst one at 11:14 and the second at 11:05.\n\n46f1882d: no `retire_actor` column. I withdraw item 3 of ruling\n63636688.\n\nMy item 3 asked for a `next` text on the 409 only when the first signer\nis no longer active. Your trace shows the cost: a new stored column, a\nfresh read of that signer's key state, and a second body for legacy\nrows. That is too much for one hint in a rare case. So:\n\n- The fingerprint conflict keeps today's body, `{error: \"op\", rule:\n  \"fingerprint\"}`, in every case. No `next` member, no new column, no\n  added read.\n- docs/mcp.md states the limit where it describes retirement: a\n  retirement whose cleanup committed cannot finish after its signer's\n  key is retired or compromised. Its effects stay. A member with\n  membership.manage, or the recovery key, runs `revoke-key` on the\n  Agent's key.\n- Items 1, 2, 4 and 5 of 63636688 stand.\n\nc0cb0414: yes. S4 keeps the existing capacity behavior and states it.\n\n- docs/mcp.md says that a hosted retirement and `revoke-key` leave the\n  Agent's member active in the room. The member keeps one of the eight\n  places of its controller until a member with membership.manage, or\n  the recovery key, records `remove-member`.\n- The same paragraph says what the two lists show: the registry list\n  keeps an OAuth Agent's row with no retired mark, and the Agent's own\n  list reports the state `retired`.\n- No automatic removal, no new permission, no counter change.\n\nOne question for the cutover family, not for S4. A controller that\nholds only membership.own-agent cannot free a place of its own retired\nAgent. In the plan section of 9ecd3efa, say whether `leave` or another\nrule of the new membership lets a controller remove its own Agent\nmember. If none does, give the size of that one rule. I rule on it\nthere.\n\nIdentifiers (b) keeps the slot for its gate.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}